  "sources": [
    {
      "title": "Coal India's October supplies cross 70 MT for first time, hit new record",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/industry/news/coal-india-s-october-supplies-cross-70-mt-for-first-time-hit-new-record-126110100720_1.html",
      "description": "Coal India's October supplies rose 19 per cent to a record 70.3 MT, with power-sector deliveries hitting an all-time high of 57.1 MT amid rising demand and lower coal imports",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-11-01T14:35:56"
    },
    {
      "title": "Coal India's October coal output rises 19.3% to 67.2 million tonnes",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/industry/news/coal-india-s-october-coal-output-rises-19-3-to-67-2-million-tonnes-126110100362_1.html",
      "description": "Coal India Ltd (CIL) on Sunday said its coal production rose 19.3 per cent year-on-year to 67.2 million tonnes (MT) in October.\nThe state-owned miner had produced 56.4 MT in the same month last year, according to provisional data filed with the stock exchanges.\nOfftake in October stood at 70.3 MT, up 18.7 per cent from 59.2 MT a year ago.\nFor the April-October period of 2026-27, CIL's production inched up 0.7 per cent to 388.2 MT from 385.5 MT in the year-ago period. Offtake during the seven months grew 9.2 per cent to 454.6 MT from 416.2 MT.\nAmong the subsidiaries, Mahanadi Coalfields Ltd (MCL) recorded the highest production in October at 20.8 MT, up 25.3 per cent. South Eastern Coalfields Ltd (SECL) produced 14.3 MT, a rise of 17.4 per cent.\nWestern Coalfields Ltd (WCL) posted the sharpest growth, with output rising 64.7 per cent to 5.9 MT. Eastern Coalfields Ltd (ECL) produced 4.6 MT, up 27.9 per cent, while Central Coalfields Ltd (CCL) output rose 26.1 per cent to 7.3 MT.\nBh",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-11-01T09:24:32"
    },
    {
      "title": "Coal imports by imported fuel-based power plants increase marginally in Aug",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/industry/news/coal-imports-by-imported-fuel-based-power-plants-increase-marginally-in-aug-126110100334_1.html",
      "description": "Coal imports by thermal power plants that depend on imported fuel rose marginally to 18.31 MT in August 2026 from 18.13 MT in the same month last year, even as the government continues to push for lower import dependence in the power sector.\nThese plants are designed to operate primarily on imported coal.\nHowever, the country's total coal import dropped by 5.83 per cent in August to 104.57 million tonnes (MT) from 111.05 MT in August last fiscal.\nThe decline reflects the continued efforts of the coal ministry to enhance domestic coal production, improve availability and strengthen the supply chain, with a view to reducing the country's dependence on imported coal.\nCoal imports by power plants dropped by 6.39 per cent to 21.32 MT in August from 22.77 MT in August 2025, the coal ministry said in a statement.\nThe decline is consistent with efforts to improve domestic coal availability and reduce dependence on imported coal for blending at domestic coal-based power plants.\nThe share",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-11-01T09:05:58"
    }
  ]
